"use strict";(self.webpackChunk_N_E=self.webpackChunk_N_E||[]).push([[8970],{8970:function(e){e.exports=JSON.parse('{"testId":"biochemistry-test-2","slug":"biochemistry","subject":"Biochemistry","testNo":2,"durationMin":40,"questions":[{"n":1,"subject":"Biochemistry","text":"A young man with chronic bacterial prostatitis was treated with cotrimoxazole. During treatment, he developed malaise, jaundice, abdominal pain, and dark urine. His blood picture is given below. What is the most likely diagnosis?","options":["Glucose-6 phosphate dehydrogenase deficiency","Paroxysmal nocturnal hemoglobinuria","Hemolytic uremic syndrome","Autoimmune hemolytic anemia"],"correct":0,"explanation":"Correct answer: A. Glucose-6 phosphate dehydrogenase deficiency\\n\\nThe most likely diagnosis in the above case scenario is glucose-6-phosphate dehydrogenase (G6PD) deficiency.\\n\\nThe clinical features are suggestive of an acute hemolytic reaction following the administration of cotrimoxazole.\xa0A hemolytic attack starts with abrupt onset of malaise, weakness, and abdominal or lumbar pain. After an interval of several hours to 2–3 days, the patient develops jaundice and often dark urine, due to hemoglobinuria.\\n\\nThe peripheral smear shows characteristic \\"bite cells\\" or degmacytes or blister cells. In G6PD deficiency\xa0uncontrolled oxidative stress causes hemoglobin to denature and form Heinz bodies. Bite cells result from\xa0the removal of Heinz bodies by macrophages in the spleen.\\n\\nCotrimoxazole contains sulfamethoxazole which triggers a hemolytic reaction in people with G6PD deficiency.\\n\\nDrugs that carry a risk of clinical hemolysis in persons with G6PD Deficiency:\\n\\nAntimalarials\\n\\nPrimaquine\\n\\nChloroquine\\n\\nDapsone/chlorproguanil\\n\\nSulphonamides/\\n\\nsulphones\\n\\nSulfamethoxazole\\n\\nSulfasalazine\\n\\nSulfisoxazole\\n\\nAntibacterial/\\n\\nantibiotics\\n\\nCotrimoxazole\\n\\nCiprofloxacin\\n\\nChloramphenicol\\n\\nNalidixic acid\\n\\nNorfloxacin\\n\\nNitrofurantoin\\n\\nOthers\\n\\nNaphthalene\\n\\nMethylene blue\\n\\nRasburicase","img":false,"explImg":false,"images":["/simg/e29bb12025c97f88acf3.jpg"]},{"n":2,"subject":"Biochemistry","text":"An elderly patient on a vegetarian diet presenting with easy fatigability and pallor was investigated. Peripheral smear showed findings of macrocytic anemia. Investigations reveal elevated serum homocysteine levels and normal serum methylmalonic acid levels. What is the probable cause of anemia in this patient?","options":["Vitamin B9 deficiency","Vitamin B12 deficiency","Vitamin B1 deficiency","Vitamin B6 deficiency"],"correct":0,"explanation":"Correct answer: A. Vitamin B9 deficiency\\n\\nThe given clinical scenario of megaloblastic anemia in a vegetarian with elevated homocysteine levels and normal serum methylmalonic acid (MMA) levels is due to folic acid deficiency.\\n\\nFolic acid (Vitamin B9) is a water-soluble vitamin, and its active form is tetrahydrofolate (THFA). It acts as a carrier of\xa0one-carbon groups.\xa0THFA is involved in the formation of many coenzymes in metabolic systems, particularly for purine and pyrimidine synthesis, nucleoprotein synthesis, and maintenance in erythropoiesis.\\n\\nFolate deficiency results in megaloblastic anemia, homocysteinemia, and neural tube defects (spina bifida, anencephaly) in the fetus.\\n\\nFolic acid deficiency is associated with the following clinical and laboratory findings:\\n\\n• Raised MCV (normal range: 80–100 fL)\xa0- macrocytosis\\n• Decrease in hemoglobin and hematocrit levels.\\n• Normal MMA level\\n• Elevated homocysteine levels.\\n\\nwhere MCV\xa0is\xa0the\xa0mean corpuscular volume.\\n\\nPatients with folate deficiency should be offered supplemental folic acid for the correction of the deficiency. Typically, oral folic acid (1 to 5 mg daily) suffices to treat folate deficiency\\n\\nOther options:\\n\\nOption B:\xa0Vitamin B12 or cobalamin\xa0deficiency presents as\xa0anemia, fatigue, and pallor symptoms. Other presenting complaints may include\xa0peripheral neuropathy,\xa0glossitis, diarrhea, headaches, neuropsychiatric disturbances like\xa0dementia, and\xa0subacute combined degeneration of the spinal cord (SACD) leading to\xa0ataxia. A peripheral smear shows\xa0macrocytic anemia\xa0and\xa0hypersegmented neutrophils. Vitamin B12 deficiency is associated with raised serum methylmalonic acid (MMA) levels.\\n\\nOption C: A patient with thiamine or\xa0vitamin B1\xa0deficiency presents either\xa0dry Beriberi\xa0with\xa0predominantly neurological\xa0symptoms like\xa0neuropathy, muscle weakness, and wasting or\xa0wet Beriberi\xa0with predominant cardiac symptoms like cardiomegaly, tachycardia, high output congestive cardiac failure.\\n\\nOption D: A patient with pyridoxine or\xa0vitamin B6\xa0deficiency presents with symptoms of seborrhea, glossitis, convulsions, neuropathy, depression, confusion, and\xa0normocytic\xa0anemia.","img":false,"explImg":false},{"n":3,"subject":"Biochemistry","text":"A 55-year-old lady presented with chronic pain in the lower back and knees. On physical examination, there were black pigmentary deposits on the sclera of both eyes and on the fingers and nailbeds. The urine turned black on exposure to Benedict\'s reagent and showed large amounts of a particular metabolite which is responsible. Identify the metabolite from the image below","options":["2","3","1","4"],"correct":0,"explanation":"Correct answer: A. 2\\n\\nThe clinical vignette is suggestive of alkaptonuria,\xa0which is characterised by excretion of the metabolite\xa0homogentisate (marked as 2\xa0in the flowchart)\xa0in urine.\\n\\nAlkaptonuria is an autosomal recessive disorder of tyrosine metabolism. It occurs due to a defect in homogentisate oxidase\xa0(which\xa0oxidizes homogentisate to maleylacetoacetate) leading to the accumulation of homogentisate. The\xa0excreted homogentisate in urine makes it appear black when it gets oxidized on exposure to air or an oxidizing agent.\\n\\nAccumulation of oxidized homogentisic acid pigment (benzoquinone acetate) in joints and connective tissues leads to arthritis and connective tissue pigmentation respectively. This is known as ochronosis.\xa0Pigmentation of heart valves, larynx, tympanic membranes, and skin, with occasional renal pigment calculi, can also be seen.\\n\\nMost patients are diagnosed early based on the darkening of urine with standing or the addition of alkali. Around the age of 30 years, gray-brown pigmentation of sclera and generalized darkening of the concha, anthelix and helix of the ear is seen.Some patients present in middle age with a degenerative joint disease (ochronotic arthritis) with pain, stiffness, and limitation of motion of the lower back, hips, knees, and shoulders. Small joints are usually spared.\\n\\nTreatment:\\n\\n• Symptomatic treatment with pain medications, spinal surgery, and arthroplasty\\n• Ascorbic acid and protein restriction\\n• Nitisinone - reduces urinary excretion of homogentisic acid and delays progression of the disease.\\n\\nThe images below show pigmentation of the skin and an x-ray of the spine that shows calcification of intervertebral discs, both symptoms of ochronosis.\\n\\nImage showing darkening of urine on standing:","img":false,"explImg":true,"images":["/simg/58f3f4160f8b43b597da.jpg"],"explBlocks":[{"t":"text"},{"t":"img","src":"/simg/1e1386670a3d9af459f6.jpg"},{"t":"img","src":"/simg/d2825e7fa6a9dd113a06.jpg"},{"t":"img","src":"/simg/13d468be95bca21002fc.jpg"},{"t":"img","src":"/simg/5910f6a5c76ab3e8dabf.png"}]},{"n":4,"subject":"Biochemistry","text":"A patient on anti-tubercular treatment presents with a tingling sensation and paresthesia in the lower limbs. Her peripheral blood smear examination shows microcytic anemia. Which of the following enzyme assay is used to diagnose the deficient vitamin in this patient?","options":["RBC glutathione reductase","Histidine load test","RBC transaminase","RBC transketolase"],"correct":2,"explanation":"Correct answer: C. RBC transaminase\\n\\nThe clinical features of tingling sensation,\xa0paresthesias\xa0, and\xa0microcytic anemia in a patient on anti-tubercular treatment are suggestive of the adverse effect of isoniazid. The vitamin deficient in this patient is pyridoxine/vitamin B6.\xa0Therefore, the RBC transaminase enzyme assay is used to diagnose vitamin B6 deficiency.\\n\\nIsoniazid is the first-line antitubercular drug that inhibits the production of mycolic acid. It is a bactericidal drug against tubercle bacilli.\\n\\nPeripheral neuritis occurs due to interference with the production of active coenzyme pyridoxal phosphate from pyridoxine. Patients present with clinical features of\xa0pyridoxine/vitamin B6\xa0deficiency, which include paresthesias, numbness, and tingling sensation. other features include\xa0seborrhea, glossitis, convulsions, neuropathy, depression, confusion, and\xa0microcytic anemia.\\n\\nThe most widely used method to know the vitamin B6 nutritional status\xa0is by measuring the\xa0RBC transaminase activity.\\n\\nTherefore, pyridoxine 10 mg/day (vitamin B6) is given prophylactically along with isoniazid to prevent neurotoxicity.","img":false,"explImg":false},{"n":5,"subject":"Biochemistry","text":"A non-essential amino acid is involved in the first step of heme synthesis. Which of the following is not a derivative of this amino acid?","options":["Creatinine","Collagen","Melanin","Serine"],"correct":2,"explanation":"Correct answer: C. Melanin\\n\\nThe non-essential amino acid involved in the first step of heme synthesis is glycine. Among the given options Melanin is a derivative of tyrosine and not glycine.\\n\\nGlycine is the simplest amino acid. It is non-essential and is glucogenic. It is involved in Heme biosynthesis, which is initiated by the condensation of succinyl-CoA and glycine in the presence of pyridoxal phosphate and δ-aminolevulinate synthase (ALA synthase) to produce δ-aminolevulinate. The Other products obtained from glycine are:\\n\\n• Creatine (from glycine, arginine, and methionine)\\n• Serine (by reversal of serine hydroxymethyl transferase reaction)\\n• Collagen (every third residue is glycine)\\n• Glutathione (from glutamic acid, cysteine, and glycine)\\n• C4, C5, and N7 of the purine ring structure\\n\\nTyrosine is a precursor of melanin, thyroxine, epinephrine, norepinephrine, and dihydroxyphenylalanine (DOPA).\\n\\n• Formation of DOPA: Tyrosine is hydroxylated by tyrosinase to form dihydroxy phenylalanine or DOPA. Tyrosinase is a copper-containing monooxygenase enzyme.\\n• Formation of melanin: Tyrosinase acts on DOPA to form dopaquinone, which is then converted to indolequinone. The indolequinone is polymerized to form melanin.","img":false,"explImg":true,"explBlocks":[{"t":"text"},{"t":"img","src":"/simg/f2e6a857388429be7120.jpg"}]},{"n":6,"subject":"Biochemistry","text":"A 6-month-old infant is brought with hypotonia and loss of motor milestones. On examination, hepatosplenomegaly and macular cherry-red spot are noted. Foam cells are seen on bone marrow biopsy. Which is the most likely deficient enzyme in this patient?","options":["Sphingomyelinase","Hexosaminidase A","Glucocerebrosidase","Galactocerebrosidase"],"correct":0,"explanation":"Correct answer: A. Sphingomyelinase\\n\\nThe given clinical scenario of progressive neurodegeneration (loss of motor milestones, hypotonia) along with hepatosplenomegaly, macular cherry-red spot, and foam cells on bone marrow biopsy point to a diagnosis of Niemann-Pick disease. The enzyme deficient in this disease is sphingomyelinase.\\n\\nNiemann-Pick disease is disorder of infancy which is almost always fatal by 2-3 years of age. Although the affected infants are normal at birth, they soon present with failure to thrive, hepatosplenomegaly, and rapid psychomotor retardation. Over time, there is severe loss of motor function and deterioration of intellect and death occurs by 3 years of age.\\n\\nNiemann-Pick disease occurs due to the deficient activity of acid sphingomyelinase enzyme. There is extensive accumulation of sphingomyelin in the monocyte-macrophage system. Lipid laden foam cells are observed in bone marrow and lymph nodes. The diagnosis is confirmed by measuring the sphingomyelinase levels in the peripheral leucocytes.\\n\\nThe management of Niemann-Pick disease is mostly supportive, such as physical therapy and a feeding tube for nutrition. Olipudase alfa has been recently approved for non-CNS manifestations of Niemann-Pick disease.\\n\\nOther options:\\n\\nOption B: Hexoseaminidase A deficiency is seen in Tay-Sachs disease. While it also presents with progressive neurodegeneration and macular cherry-red spot, there is no organomegaly and lysosomes with onion skin are seen.\\n\\nOption C: Glucocerebrosidase deficiency is seen in Gaucher disease. The patients present with hematological abnormalities, organomegaly, and skeletal manifestations in the form of bone pain and pathological fractures. Bone marrow examination shows macrophages with characteristic crumbled tissue paper appearance.\\n\\nOption D: Galactocerebrosidase deficiency is seen in Krabbe disease. Both peripheral and central myelin is affected in this disease. The patients present with spasticity and intellectual impairment (destruction of central myelin) and peripheral neuropathy and absent deep tendon reflexes (destruction of peripheral myelin). Optic atrophy may also be seen.","img":false,"explImg":false},{"n":7,"subject":"Biochemistry","text":"Which of the following amino acid is likely to be most abundant in collagen?","options":["Alanine","Proline","Glycine","Lysine"],"correct":2,"explanation":"Correct answer: C. Glycine\\n\\nThe most abundant amino acid in collagen is glycine.\\n\\nCollagen is the most abundant protein in the animal world. It consists of three alpha chains held together by hydrogen bonds forming a\xa0triple helical structure.\\n\\nA striking characteristic of collagen is the occurrence of glycine residues at every third position, (Gly-X-Y) repeats.\\n\\nProline and hydroxyproline confer rigidity on the collagen molecule. Hydroxyproline and hydroxylysine are formed by the post-translational hydroxylation of proline and lysine residues. This hydroxylation is catalyzed by the enzyme prolyl and lysyl hydroxylase, and cofactors are ascorbic acid (vitamin C) and α-ketoglutarate.\\n\\nIntracellular\xa0steps in the synthesis of collagen:\\n\\n• Signal peptide cleavage\\n• Hydroxylation of prolyl residues and some lysyl residues, glycosylation of some hydroxylysyl residues\\n• Intrachain and interchain S–S bonds formation in extension peptides\\n• Triple helix formation\\n\\nExtracellular\xa0steps in the synthesis of collagen:\\n\\n• Amino- and carboxyl-terminal propeptides cleavage\\n• Collagen fibers assembly in quarter-staggered alignment\\n• Oxidative deamination of h -amino groups of lysyl and hydroxylysyl residues to aldehydes\\n• Intra- and interchain cross-links formation via Schiff bases and aldol condensation products","img":false,"explImg":false},{"n":8,"subject":"Biochemistry","text":"Which of the following conversions requires pantothenic acid as an essential cofactor?","options":["Glucose to pyruvate","Glutamate to alpha-ketoglutarate","Glucose to ribose-5-phosphate","Oxaloacetate to citrate"],"correct":3,"explanation":"Correct answer: D. Oxaloacetate to citrate\\n\\nPantothenic acid is an essential cofactor of coenzyme A which is required for the reaction involving the transfer of acyl group such as oxaloacetate to citrate.\\n\\nIn the first step of the TCA cycle, acetyl CoA combines with oxaloacetate to form citrate involving the transfer of acetyl group to oxaloacetate. Also in the same cycle, succinyl CoA synthesis from alpha-ketoglutarate requires coenzyme A.\\n\\nThere are a variety of biochemical reactions that require CoA such as fatty acid synthesis and beta-oxidation.\\n\\nDeficiency of pantothenic acid (vitamin B5) causes peripheral nerve damage also known as burning foot syndrome.\\n\\nOther options:\\n\\nOption A: Conversion of glucose to pyruvate in aerobic glycolysis requires the coenzyme NAD+ (vitamin B3). This reaction does not require coenzyme A.\\n\\nOption B: Glutamate dehydrogenase which converts glutamate to alpha-ketoglutarate utilizes NAD+ or NADP+\xa0as a coenzyme.\\n\\nOption C: Conversion of glucose to ribose-5-phosphate in the hexose monophosphate shunt requires the coenzyme NADP+ (vitamin B3).","img":false,"explImg":true,"explBlocks":[{"t":"text"},{"t":"img","src":"/simg/af92e419aefaf0c51e0a.jpg"}]},{"n":9,"subject":"Biochemistry","text":"Which of the following is associated with a defect in collagen maturity?","options":["Scurvy","Rickets","Beri-beri","Night blindness"],"correct":0,"explanation":"Correct answer: A. Scurvy\\n\\nScurvy is associated with a defect in collagen maturity.\\n\\nScurvy results from ascorbic acid\xa0or vitamin C deficiency.\xa0Vitamin C is an essential cofactor for the enzyme prolyl hydroxylase, required for the\xa0hydroxylation of prolyl residues in collagen. This enables each collagen fibril to form a uniform and flexible triple helix configuration in the body’s connective tissues.\\n\\nIts deficiency results in a defective prolyl hydroxylase activity, thereby resulting in the impaired formation of mature connective tissue; the clinical manifestations of this deficiency include\xa0petechiae, ecchymosis, coiled hairs, inflamed and bleeding gums, joint effusion, tooth loss, bone fracture,\xa0 poor wound healing, and fatigue which can be attributed to impaired collagen synthesis all of which are typical of scurvy.\\n\\nRisk factors include chronic alcoholism, diseases such as celiac disease and Crohn’s disease, associated with malabsorption and poor nutrition.\\n\\nIt is treated with vitamin C supplementation.\\n\\nOther options:\\n\\nOption B- Rickets is caused due to deficiency of vitamin D in children which is characterized by the continued formation of the collagen matrix of bone but its incomplete mineralization or demineralization. Children have soft, pliable bones.\\n\\nOption C- Beriberi is caused due to deficiency of thiamine. It can\xa0either be dry beriberi characterized by peripheral neuropathy or wet beriberi characterized by edema due to dilated cardiomyopathy.\\n\\nOption D- Night blindness is caused by a deficiency of vitamin A. Deficiency can also lead to pancreatic insufficiency, repeated gastrointestinal, pulmonary, and urinary infections, and xeropthalmia.","img":false,"explImg":false},{"n":10,"subject":"Biochemistry","text":"Which of the following is false about the given graph?","options":["Inhibitor binds to the active site","Inhibitor does not change the shape of the active site","Vmax decreases and Kmax is unchanged","Increase in substrate concentration reduces the inhibition"],"correct":2,"explanation":"Correct answer: C. Vmax decreases and Kmax is unchanged\\n\\nThe given graph shows competitive inhibition in which the value of Vmax remains constant and the Km value increases.\\n\\nWhen the active site or catalytic site of an enzyme is occupied by a substance other than the substrate of that enzyme, this type of inhibition is known as Competitive inhibition. eg: succinate dehydrogenase reaction is inhibited by malonate. The inhibitor binds to the active site. The inhibitor does not change the shape of the active site. Competitive Inhibition is reversible.\\n\\nLineweaver Burk Plot for other enzyme inhibition,","img":false,"explImg":true,"images":["/simg/d603aaf97739bfe03d09.png"],"explBlocks":[{"t":"text"},{"t":"img","src":"/simg/68e43b691b76e93bdff0.jpg"},{"t":"img","src":"/simg/dd5e4b71238f574d2ae3.jpg"}]},{"n":11,"subject":"Biochemistry","text":"Which of the following statement is false regarding epigenetic modification:","options":["The acetylation of histone favors active transcription","DNA methylation is restricted between cytosine and guanine residues connected by hydrogen bonds","It causes the alteration of gene expression without mutation","Genomic imprinting is a phenomenon involved in epigenetic gene inactivation"],"correct":1,"explanation":"Correct answer: B. DNA methylation is restricted between cytosine and guanine residues connected by hydrogen bonds\\n\\nDNA methylation usually occurs between cytosine and guanine residues connected by phosphodiester bond(CpG) island on the same strand.\\n\\nEpigenetics is defined as heritable chemical changes in gene expression that are not caused by the\xa0alteration in the gene sequence. It alters gene expression without the mutation. Epigenetic modification can cause both gene activation and inactivation. If it causes gene inactivation, it is known as Genomic imprinting.\\n\\nThe epigenetic modification includes:\\n\\n• DNA methylation is usually restricted to cytosines of CpG dinucleotides, which are abundant throughout the genome. CpG islands in promoter regions are typically unmethylated, and the lack of methylation facilitates transcription.\\n• Acetylation and deacetylation of lysine residues in core histones which activates and deactivates gene expression respectively. Acetylation of histone favors active transcription.\\n• Methylation of histones: Histone Methylation Mostly Makes DNA Mute.","img":false,"explImg":false},{"n":12,"subject":"Biochemistry","text":"Match the following urine tests with respective substances they detect.\\n\\nTest\\n\\nSubstance\\n\\n1. Rothera test\\n\\nA. Urobilinogen\\n\\n2. Benedict\'s test\\n\\nB. Bile salts\\n\\n3. Hay\'s test\\n\\nC. Glucose\\n\\n4. Ehrlich\'s test\\n\\nD. Acetoacetate","options":["1-D, 2-C, 3-B, 4-A","1-D, 2-A, 3-B, 4-C","1-D, 2-C, 3-A, 4-B","1-A, 2-C, 3-B, 4-D"],"correct":0,"explanation":"Correct answer: A. 1-D, 2-C, 3-B, 4-A\\n\\nThe correctly matched option is\xa01-D, 2-C, 3-B,\xa04-A.\\n\\nRothera’s test\xa0by\xa0nitroprusside reagent\xa0is a specific test used to detect whether\xa0acetone\xa0or\xa0acetoacetate\xa0is present in the given sample of urine. A\xa0purple ring\xa0at the junction of two liquids indicates a positive test. Rothera\'s test does\xa0not detect\xa0β-hydroxybutyrate.\\n\\nThe other test used for the purpose of detecting acetoacetate only is the ferric chloride test. It shows a burgundy red color that is graded from +1 to +4. False-positive results occur with drugs like salicylates and phenols.\\n\\nThe image below shows the purple ring formed by a positive Rothera test.\\n\\nBenedict\'s test is a test commonly used in the follow-up of diabetics for the detection of glucose in urine (glucosuria). It uses Benedict\'s reagent containing sodium carbonate, copper sulfate, and sodium citrate. Reducing sugars (containing a free aldehyde/ keto group) will reduce Benedict\'s reagent. Examples include glucose, fructose, and lactose.\\n\\nThe test is a semi-quantitative test. The blue color indicates no reducing sugar.\\n\\nColor\\nConcentration\\n\\nGreen\\n0.5 g%\\n\\nYellow\\n1 g%\\n\\nOrange\\n1.5 g%\\n\\nRed\\n2 g%\\n\\nHay\'s test is used for the detection of bile salts in\xa0urine.\xa0Normally, bile salts (sodium salts of taurocholic acid and glycocholic acid) are not present in urine. Thus, positive Hay\'s test indicates an obstructive pathology in the biliary system, leading to the backflow of bile into the circulation. This obstruction may be associated with either obstructive jaundice or hepatic jaundice.\\n\\nEhrlich\'s test\xa0is used to determine urinary\xa0and fecal urobilinogen.\\n\\nPorphobilinogens form a red aldehyde compound\xa0with Ehrlich reagent, which is insoluble in chloroform. A red color remaining after chloroform extraction is indicative of porphobilinogens. This test is\xa0positive in acute intermittent porphyria and hemolytic jaundice. It is negative in hepatic jaundice (later stages) and obstructive jaundice. The earliest sign of recovery in obstructive jaundice is the re-appearance of urobilinogen in urine.\\n\\nNormal color of urine is\xa0pale straw\xa0due to presence of\xa0urochrome.\\n\\nAbnormality\\nCause\\n\\nSmokey or cloudy\\n\\n• AGN\\n• Nephrotic syndrome\\n\\nGross blood\\n\\n• Urological causes\\n• Nephro causes\\n• Hematological causes, e.g. PNH\\n\\nRed without blood [brownish tinge]\\n\\n• Rhabdomyolysis\\n• Beetroot ingestion\\n\\nTea- or cola-colored urine\\nHemoglobinuria\\n\\nHigh-colored urine\\nBilirubinuria\\n\\nDarkens on standing\\nAlkaptonuria\\n\\nDue to Drugs:\\n\\nAbnormality\\nDrugs\\n\\nOrange\\n\\n• Rifamycins (rifampicin, rifabutin, rifapentine)\\n• B-complex\\n\\nBlue\\n\\n• Amitriptyline\\n• Methylene blue\\n• Copper poisoning\\n\\nBrown\\n\\n• Chloroquine\\n• Quinine\\n• Primaquine\\n• Nitrofurantoin\\n\\nYellow\\nTetracycline\\n\\nPinkish\\nDeferoxamine\\n\\nGreen\\nTriamterene\\n\\nDarkens on standing\\n\\n• Metronidazole\\n• Methyldopa\\n• Imipenem-cilastin","img":false,"explImg":true,"explBlocks":[{"t":"text"},{"t":"img","src":"/simg/342a3963e3bf35dfc86f.jpg"},{"t":"img","src":"/simg/4e8399aec06354e9c456.jpg"}]},{"n":13,"subject":"Biochemistry","text":"Which anticoagulant present in the light blue vacutainer is used for plasma glucose estimation?","options":["Potassium oxalate","Sodium citrate","Heparin","Sodium fluoride"],"correct":1,"explanation":"Correct answer: B. Sodium citrate\\n\\nLight blue vacutainers contain the anticoagulant sodium citrate.\\n\\nVacutainer\\nAdditive\\nUses\\n\\nLight grey\\nSodium fluoride and potassium oxalate\\nBlood glucose estimation\\n\\nLight green\\nLithium Heparin\\nPlasma testing\\n\\nLight blue\\nSodium citrate\\nCoagulation tests (APTT and\\nprothrombin time)\\n\\nCitrated plasma generated from whole blood is commonly utilized in laboratory testing for hemostasis. The recommended procedure involves the collection of specimens in tubes that are filled with a solution containing 3.2% sodium citrate (109 mM). The appropriate ratio for this collection is 9 parts blood to 1 part anticoagulant. The primary objective of citrate is to effectively eliminate calcium ions, which play a crucial role in the process of blood coagulation.\\n\\nThe following image shows color coding of vacutainers with the anticoagulant used and their practical uses:","img":false,"explImg":true,"explBlocks":[{"t":"text"},{"t":"img","src":"/simg/a41901e66b567c55abbb.jpg"}]},{"n":14,"subject":"Biochemistry","text":"Which of the following is the earliest feature in vitamin A deficiency?","options":["Keratomalacia","Loss of sensitivity to green light","Xerophthalmia","Night blindness"],"correct":1,"explanation":"Correct answer: B. Loss of sensitivity to green light\\n\\nLoss of sensitivity to green light is the earliest feature of vitamin A deficiency.\\n\\nVitamin A is a\xa0fat-soluble\xa0vitamin that can occur as\xa0carotenoids\xa0(most common - beta carotene from plant sources) or\xa0retinoids (from animal sources). Dietary sources of vitamin A are liver, fish, eggs, dark green, and deeply coloured fruits and vegetables.\\n\\n3 forms of retinoids are:\\n\\n• Retinal\xa0is required mainly for normal vision (present in the visual pigment rhodopsin).\\n• Retinol\xa0is important for the normal functioning of the reproductive system.\\n• Retinoic acid\xa0has a role in the regulation of gene expression and tissue differentiation.\\n\\nThe sequence of visual defects in vitamin A deficiency:\\n\\n1. Impairment of the eye to adapt to dim light\\n2. Night blindness\\n3. Bitot spots on the conjunctiva -\xa0white patches of keratinized epithelium on the sclera\\n4. Prolonged deficiency of vitamin A → xerophthalmia (dryness of the conjunctiva and cornea)\\n5. If xerophthalmia is not treated, it results in keratomalacia (softening of the cornea),\xa0corneal ulceration, corneal scarring\xa0and ultimately complete blindness.\\n\\nThe image below shows a Bitot\'s spot which is pathognomic\xa0of vitamin A deficiency.\\n\\nOther manifestations of vitamin A deficiency:\\n\\n• General impairment of immunity\\n• Keratinization of skin (phrynoderma/toad skin)","img":false,"explImg":true,"explBlocks":[{"t":"text"},{"t":"img","src":"/simg/1af6885426d39c1a67e3.jpg"}]},{"n":15,"subject":"Biochemistry","text":"A patient presented with tendon xanthomas. His lipid profile showed serum cholesterol levels to be 283 mg/dL and serum LDL to be 202 mg/dL. He was started on lovastatin. What is the likely diagnosis?\\n\\nLDL - Low density lipoprotein","options":["Familial hypercholesterolemia","Tangier\'s disease","Lipoprotein lipase deficiency","Familial hyperchylomicronemia"],"correct":0,"explanation":"Correct answer: A. Familial hypercholesterolemia\\n\\nThe clinical presentation with tendon xanthomas and elevated serum LDL (low-density lipoprotein) and elevated serum cholesterol points to a diagnosis of familial hypercholesterolemia (FH).\\n\\nFamilial hypercholesterolemia \xa0(type IIa hyperlipoproteinemia) is an inherited disorder due to defects in LDL receptors. This defect causes a decrease in the uptake of LDL and cholesterol. As a result, the blood levels of both LDL and cholesterol are elevated. Such patients are at increased risk to develop atherosclerosis and coronary artery disease.\\n\\nFeatures of FH:\\n\\n• Painful polyarthritis and tendinitis are common.\\n• Physical findings seen are corneal arcus\xa0and\xa0tendon xanthomas over Achilles tendon and\xa0the dorsum of hands. Xanthomas are tumorous masses produced by foamy cell clusters (lipid-laden macrophages) found in the connective tissue of the skin and in tendons.\\n\\nDiagnosis is done clinically based on hypercholesterolemia with\\n\\n• LDL > 190 mg/dl,\\n• Family history of hypercholesterolemia and/or\\n• Premature coronary artery disease.\\n\\nTreatment includes lifestyle changes, restriction of a fatty diet, and pharmacotherapy with statins in heterozygous FH patients.\\n\\nRecent drugs for specific treatment of homozygous FH are:\\n\\n• Lomitapide, a microsomal triglyceride transfer protein (MTP) inhibitor, and\\n• Mipomersen, an antisense oligonucleotide against apoB.\\n\\nThe image below shows Achilles tendon xanthoma and a plain lateral radiograph of both ankles showing bilateral symmetrical swellings (arrows) superior to the calcaneum respectively.\\n\\nOther options\\n\\nOption B: Tangier’s disease is a hypolipoproteinemia caused by a mutation of the ABCA1 gene. Enlarged orange tonsils are pathognomonic. Low HDL levels are seen. There is an increased risk for atherosclerosis.\\n\\nThe image below shows enlarged yellow-orange colored tonsils in Tangier\'s disease.\\n\\nOption C: Lipoprotein lipase (LPL) deficiency causes type I hyperlipoproteinemia due to defects in LPL or apo C-II. Eruptive xanthomas are seen. Chylomicrons and VLDL are elevated. LDL and HDL are low. There is no risk for coronary disease.\\n\\nOption D: Familial hyperchylomicronemia syndrome is caused due to either\xa0LPL deficiency,\xa0ApoC-II deficiency,\xa0ApoA-V deficiency,\xa0or\xa0GPIHBP1 mutation.\xa0Clinically, patients present with\xa0recurrent episodes of severe abdominal pain due to\xa0acute pancreatitis.\xa0The retinal blood vessels may be opalescent and are called\xa0lipemia retinalis. Increased levels of triglycerides (mostly >1000 mg/dL) is seen. The risk of premature coronary heart disease is low.","img":false,"explImg":true,"explBlocks":[{"t":"text"},{"t":"img","src":"/simg/b841f2be22262240f3d9.png"},{"t":"img","src":"/simg/87666e972893b94499e7.png"},{"t":"img","src":"/simg/c034786cba13b2aa7b3e.jpg"}]},{"n":16,"subject":"Biochemistry","text":"A 32-year-old man is found to have right-sided colon cancer. His father had similar colon cancer and died at the age of 40. His paternal aunt suffers from endometrial cancer and his paternal uncle also suffers from urothelial cancer. There is no history of familial adenomatous polyposis. Abnormality in which of the following DNA repair mechanisms is responsible for this patient\'s condition?","options":["Nucleotide excision repair","Homologous recombination","Base excision repair","Mismatch repair"],"correct":3,"explanation":"Correct answer: D. Mismatch repair\\n\\nThe given clinical scenario is suggestive of Lynch syndrome or\xa0hereditary nonpolyposis colorectal cancer (HNPCC) and mismatch repair is\xa0the defective DNA repair mechanism here.\\n\\nHNPCC is an autosomal dominant genetic condition that is associated with a high risk of colon cancer\xa0as well as other cancers including endometrial cancer (second most common), cancers of the stomach, ovary, ureters, etc. It is the most common cause of the syndromic form of colon cancer. Colon cancers are seen at younger ages and usually on the right side.\\n\\nDNA mismatch repair proteins\xa0function as spell checkers\xa0during DNA replication by correcting the\xa0incorrect pairing.\xa0In HNPCC, the proofreading function of the mismatch repair systems (majorly\xa0MLH1\xa0and\xa0MSH2)\xa0is lost leading to the accumulation of errors throughout the genome.\xa0The genomes of these patients show microsatellite instability,\xa0which is characterized by changes in the length of short repeats.\\n\\nOther options:\\n\\nOption A: Defects in the\xa0nucleotide excision repair\xa0pathway are seen in patients with\xa0xeroderma pigmentosum.\\n\\nOption B: Defects in\xa0non-homologous end-joining are seen in patients with subacute combined immunodeficiency syndrome\xa0(SCID).\\n\\nOption C: Defects in DNA base excision repair are seen in patients with MUTYH-associated polyposis (MAP) syndromes.","img":false,"explImg":false},{"n":17,"subject":"Biochemistry","text":"A 9-year-old boy was brought with complaints of a fishy smell in his hands, feet, saliva, and urine. A urinalysis revealed the substance responsible for the odor, and a diagnosis of Fish odor syndrome was made. Which is the substance responsible for this characteristic body odor?","options":["Trimethylamine-N-oxide","Trimethylamine","Flavin monooxygenase","Choline"],"correct":1,"explanation":"Correct answer: B. Trimethylamine\\n\\nIn the given clinical scenario of fish odor syndrome, the characteristic odor is due to\xa0trimethylamine (TMA), which is released in sweat, urine, and breath.\\n\\nFish odour syndrome\xa0(trimethylaminuria) is an autosomal recessive disease. It is due to a deficiency of\xa0flavin-containing monooxygenase 3 (FMO3) which converts the amino acid, TMA (trimethylamine) to TMA-N-oxide (which is\xa0non-odorous) in the liver. TMA thus builds up in the body and is released in sweat, urine, and breath, giving off a strong odor of rotting fish from the body.\\n\\nPatients present in childhood or early adulthood with halitosis or body odor (distinctive fishy odor). Patients suffer from psychosocial problems such as shame, anxiety, depression, etc. Diagnosis is based on high levels of excreted trimethylamine (TMA) in the patient\'s urine.\\n\\nTreatment involves avoiding foods containing choline such as egg, liver, peas, and soy products. A short course of antibiotics such as neomycin, and metronidazole can be given which reduce the production of trimethylamine by the gut bacteria.","img":false,"explImg":false},{"n":18,"subject":"Biochemistry","text":"Which Vitamin forms a part of Coenzyme A and acyl carrier protein (ACP)?","options":["Vitamin B2","Vitamin B3","Vitamin B5","Vitamin B6"],"correct":2,"explanation":"Correct answer: C. Vitamin B5\\n\\nPantothenic Acid (Vitamin B5) acts as the pantetheine functional moiety in Coenzyme A and\xa0acyl carrier protein (ACP)\\n\\nVitamin B5 plays a central role in acyl group metabolism.\\n\\nFunctions of CoA (Coenzyme A) :\\n\\nTakes part in the reactions of\\n\\n• Citric acid cycle\\n• Fatty acid oxidation\\n• Acetylations\\n• Cholesterol synthesis\\n\\nACP plays a major role in Fatty Acid synthesis\\n\\nOther options :\\n\\nOption A: Riboflavin (Vitamin B2) is responsible for the coenzymes flavin mononucleotide (FMN) and Flavin-adenine-dinucelotide (FAD)\\n\\nOption B: Niacin (Vitamin B3) helps in the formation of coenzymes Nicotinamide-adenine dinucleotide (NAD) and NADP\\n\\nOption D: Pyridoxine (Vitamin B6) forms Pyridoxal Phosphate (PLP) as a coenzyme.","img":false,"explImg":false},{"n":19,"subject":"Biochemistry","text":"A man presented with slurred speech and festinating shuffling gait. He tells that a course of levodopa was advised by his previous doctor but didn\'t show any effect. Which of the following mineral toxicity/ deficiency causes such features?","options":["Selenium","Zinc","Manganese","Magnesium"],"correct":2,"explanation":"Correct answer: C. Manganese\\n\\nManganese toxicity can cause\xa0secondary parkinsonism\xa0with features similar to atypical parkinsonism.\\n\\nSecondary parkinsonism is characterized by Parkinson-like disease caused by agents/factors that cause basal ganglia dysfunction. These factors include drugs (tetrabenazine, metoclopramide, neuroleptics, flunarizine), stroke, tumor, infection, or toxins.\xa0Clinical features will be based on the region of the basal ganglia that has been damaged.\\n\\nToxins such as carbon monoxide or manganese produce\xa0damage to the globus pallidus, and hence present with features resembling\xa0atypical parkinsonism\xa0such as:\\n\\n• Early speech and gait impairment\\n• Repeated early falls\\n• Absence of rest tremor\\n• No motor asymmetry\\n• Poor or no response to levodopa\\n• Aggressive clinical course\\n• Symptoms of severe early autonomic involvement\\n\\nManganese\xa0deficiency on the other hand can result in poor growth,\xa0bone demineralization, ataxia, convulsions, and abnormalities in carbohydrate and lipid metabolism.\\n\\nOther options\\n\\nOption A: Selenium deficiency causes Keshan disease (cardiomyopathy in endemic regions of China); Excess selenium intake leads to selenosis\xa0(with hair loss, brittle nails, garlic breath odor, myopathy, and neurologic symptoms).\\n\\nOption B: Zinc toxicity leads to nausea, vomiting, headache, fever, and hypersalivation. Zinc deficiency causes dwarfism, hypogonadism, hypopigmented hair, hypogeusia, and impaired immunity.\\n\\nOption D: Hypomagnesemia is characterized by neurologic symptoms like tremors, muscle weakness, ataxia, delirium, and psychosis. Hypermagnesemia causes lethargy, weakness, hypotonia, weak tendon reflexes, and coma.","img":false,"explImg":false},{"n":20,"subject":"Biochemistry","text":"Which of the following steps in the metabolism of Vitamin D requires sunlight?","options":["25-hydroxycholecalciferol to 1,25 dihydroxycholecalciferol","1,25 dihydroxycholecalciferol to 7-dehydrocholesterol","Cholecalciferol to 25-hydroxycholecalciferol","7-dehydrocholesterol to cholecalciferol"],"correct":3,"explanation":"Correct answer: D. 7-dehydrocholesterol to cholecalciferol\\n\\nIn the metabolism of vitamin D, the conversion of 7-dehydrocholesterol\xa0to cholecalciferol requires sunlight.\\n\\nVitamin D metabolism occurs in 3 steps:\\n\\nStep 1-\xa0Skin: 7-dehydrocholesterol (an intermediate in cholesterol synthesis that accumulates in the skin) undergoes a nonenzymic reaction on exposure to ultraviolet light, yielding\xa0cholecalciferol, which is absorbed into the bloodstream. Note that\xa07-Dehydrocholesterol\xa0and cholecalciferol already contain one hydroxy group.\\n\\nStep 2-\xa0Liver:\xa0\xa0Cholecalciferol is hydroxylated to\xa025-hydroxycholecalciferol.\xa0This is released into the circulation bound to a vitamin D–binding globulin, which is the main storage form of the vitamin.\\n\\nStep 3- Kidney:\xa025-hydroxycholecalciferol\xa0is converted to\xa01,25 dihydroxycholecalciferol, the biologically active form\xa0by the enzyme\xa01α-hydroxylase.","img":false,"explImg":true,"explBlocks":[{"t":"text"},{"t":"img","src":"/simg/e98053e324b90409662c.jpg"}]},{"n":21,"subject":"Biochemistry","text":"Where does the Krebs–Henseleit cycle cycle take place?","options":["Cytoplasm and mitochondria","Mitochondria","Nucleus","Cytoplasm"],"correct":0,"explanation":"Correct answer: A. Cytoplasm and mitochondria\\n\\nOrnithine cycle/Krebs–Henseleit cycle/Urea cycle occurs in both the\xa0cytoplasm and mitochondria.\\n\\nThe reactions that take place both in the cytoplasm and mitochondria are:\\n\\n• H: Heme synthesis\\n• U: Urea cycle\\n• G: Gluconeogenesis\\n• Pathway:\xa0Pyrimidine synthesis\\n\\nUrea cycle disorders are all characterized by hyperammonemia, encephalopathy, and respiratory alkalosis. This is due to an accumulation of the precursors of urea, namely ammonia and glutamine.\\n\\nThe enzymes involved in the urea cycle with their related disorders are given below:\\n\\n• Carbamoyl-phosphate synthetase (CPS) 1 deficiency (hyperammonemia type 1) - rare and characterized by severe hyperammonemia.\\n• Ornithine transporter deficiency (HHH syndrome) - is characterized by hyperornithinemia, hyperammonemia, and homocitrullinuria.\\n• Ornithine transcarbamylase (OTC) deficiency (hyperammonemia type 2) - is inherited as an X-linked trait. Characterized by hyperammonemia and elevated glutamine levels in blood and CSF.\\n• Argininosuccinate synthetase deficiency - is characterized by increased citrulline, citrullinuria, and hyperammonemia.\\n• Argininosuccinate lyase deficiency- leads to argininosuccinic acidemia and metabolic acidosis. Hyperammonemia is less severe, and argininosuccinate is found in urine and CSF.\\n• Arginase deficiency- mild variety. Characterized by hyperargininemia and argininuria.\\n\\nClinical features of urea cycle disorders are common to all and include vomiting, avoidance of high-protein foods, intermittent ataxia, irritability, lethargy, and severe mental retardation.\\n\\nLong terms treatment consists of a protein-restricted diet.","img":false,"explImg":false},{"n":22,"subject":"Biochemistry","text":"If the percentage of thymine residues in DNA is 28%. What is the percentage of cytosine?","options":["36%","28%","44%","22%"],"correct":3,"explanation":"Correct answer: D. 22%\\n\\nThe percentage of cytosine in the DNA is 22%.\\n\\nAs per the Chargaff\'s rule, in the DNA molecules, the concentration of deoxyadenosine (A) equals that of thymidine (T), and the concentration of deoxyguanosine (G) equals that of deoxycytidine (C).\xa0Therefore,\xa0A=T and G=C.\\n\\nIn the question, the concentration of thymine is given as 28%, therefore the concentration of adenine is also 28%, both of which add up to 56%.\xa0The remaining 44% (100-56) of the DNA consists of G-C base pairs. Of which the guanine constitutes 22% and the cytosine constitutes another 22%.","img":false,"explImg":false},{"n":23,"subject":"Biochemistry","text":"A neonate presents with feeding difficulty and failure to thrive. Her urine has a peculiar burnt sugar odor due to an excess of branched-chain amino acids. Which of the following is unlikely to be elevated?","options":["Lysine","Isoleucine","Valine","Leucine"],"correct":0,"explanation":"Correct answer: A. Lysine\\n\\nThe given clinical scenario is suggestive of maple syrup urine disease (MSUD). Lysine is not a branched-chain amino acid and hence is unlikely to be elevated.\\n\\nBranched-chain amino acids are:\\n\\n• Valine (V)\\n• Isoleucine (I)\\n• Leucine (L)\\n\\nMSUD\xa0is due to\xa0the deficiency\xa0of branched-chain alpha-keto acid dehydrogenase complex (BCKDC), leading to the formation of branched-chain amino acids and their toxic by-products (ketoacids) in the blood and urine. There is a peculiar odor of\xa0burnt sugar\xa0found in\xa0urine,\xa0sweat, and\xa0cerumen.","img":false,"explImg":false},{"n":24,"subject":"Biochemistry","text":"A 6-year-old girl is brought with a history of impaired vision. On examination, she has light hair, light skin color, and pale blue iris. The child has a normal IQ. Which of the following are not derived from the precursor amino acid, whose metabolism is impaired in this child?\\n\\nDOPA- dihydroxyphenylalanine, GABA- Gamma-aminobutyric acid","options":["DOPA","Epinephrine","Thyroxine","GABA"],"correct":3,"explanation":"Correct answer: D. GABA\\n\\nThe given scenario of a child with impaired vision, light hair, light skin color, and pale blue iris with a normal IQ is suggestive of albinism, which is a disorder of tyrosine metabolism where there is a defect in melanin synthesis. Tyrosine is not the precursor amino acid of GABA (γ-aminobutyric acid); it is glutamate.\\n\\nTyrosine is a precursor of melanin, thyroxin, epinephrine, norepinephrine, and dihydroxyphenylalanine (DOPA).\\n\\n• Formation of DOPA: Tyrosine is hydroxylated by tyrosinase to form dihydroxy phenylalanine or DOPA. Tyrosinase is a monooxygenase that contains copper.\\n• Formation of melanin: Tyrosinase acts on DOPA to form dopaquinone, which is then converted to indolequinone. The indolequinone is polymerized to form melanin.\\n• Formation of catecholamines: Epinephrine, norepinephrine, and dopamine. They are produced by the adrenal medulla and sympathetic ganglia.\\n• Formation of thyroid hormones: Tyrosine residues are iodinated to form 3-mono-iodo-tyrosine (MIT) and 3,5-di-iodo-tyrosine (DIT). These are coupled to give rise to triiodothyronine (T3) and tetraiodothyronine\xa0(thyroxine or T4).\\n\\nThe series of reactions involving the production of catecholamines is shown in the image below.\\n\\nOculocutaneous albinism\xa0(OCA)\xa0is a group of inherited disorders characterized by a reduction or complete lack of\xa0melanin\xa0pigment in the\xa0skin, hair,\xa0and\xa0eyes leading to hypopigmentation. It is either due to defective melanosomes or defective protein involved in the formation and transfer of melanin.\\n\\nThere are 7 types of\xa0nonsyndromic albinism (OCA1 to OCA7).\xa0OCA type 1 is characterized by reduced or absent\xa0tyrosinase activity.\xa0Clinical features include\xa0white hair,\xa0white skin,\xa0and\xa0iris\xa0appearing\xa0blue-grey\xa0in oblique light. Ocular manifestations include\xa0photophobia,\xa0nystagmus, and\xa0decreased visual acuity.\xa0The\xa0IQ\xa0of the child will be\xa0normal.","img":false,"explImg":true,"explBlocks":[{"t":"text"},{"t":"img","src":"/simg/50bc0a8e67474b4b9586.jpg"}]},{"n":25,"subject":"Biochemistry","text":"A young chemist notices a reaction system in which the rate of conversion of substrates to product is equal to the rate at which products are converted back to substrates. Which of the following properties regarding this reaction system is true?","options":["The equilibrium constant is a ratio of the reaction rates","The rate constants of the forward and backward reactions are equal","Equilibrium is a dynamic state","The value of equilibrium constant increases with increase in catalyst or enzyme concentration"],"correct":2,"explanation":"Correct answer: C. Equilibrium is a dynamic state\\n\\nAmong the given options, Equilibrium is a dynamic state\xa0is the correct option.\\n\\nIn an equilibrium state, Although there is no net change in the concentration of substrates or products, individual substrate, and product molecules are continually being interconverted and hence it is said to be dynamic.\\n\\nSome important properties of a system at equilibrium are:\\n\\n• The equilibrium constant (Keq) is a ratio of the reaction rate constants (not the reaction rates).\\n• At equilibrium, the reaction rates (not the rate constants) of the forward and back reactions are equal.\\n• The numerical value of the constant can be calculated by finding the concentrations of substrates and products.\\n• If Keq is more than 1, the forward reaction is favoured. In such instances, the reaction is spontaneous and exothermic.\\n• The concentration of the enzyme does not affect the Keq. The concentration of enzyme certainly increases the rate of reaction; but not the Keq or the ultimate state. Catalysts increase the rate of reaction but do not alter the equilibrium.","img":false,"explImg":false},{"n":26,"subject":"Biochemistry","text":"Which of the following is not a substrate for glucose formation?","options":["Acetyl coenzyme A","Alanine","Lactate","Glycerol"],"correct":0,"explanation":"Correct answer: A. Acetyl coenzyme A\\n\\nAcetyl coenzyme A (CoA) is not a substrate for glucose formation.\\n\\nAcetyl CoA and compounds that give rise to acetyl CoA alone like acetoacetate, lysine, and leucine cannot give rise to a net synthesis of glucose. This is because of the irreversible nature of the pyruvate dehydrogenase complex, which converts pyruvate to acetyl CoA. These compounds give rise to ketone bodies instead.\\n\\nThe synthesis of glucose in the body, in the absence of dietary carbohydrates, occurs in two ways:\\n\\n• From liver glycogen, by a process known as\xa0glycogenolysis in the absence of dietary carbohydrates, for up to 24 hours.\\n• De novo, from non-carbohydrate sources, by a process known as gluconeogenesis, when glycogen stores have depleted. This occurs primarily in the liver and to a lesser extent in the kidneys.\\n\\nIn gluconeogenesis, the substrates can be:\\n\\n• Oxaloacetate (from its conjugate acid, oxaloacetic acid) is a direct precursor of phosphoenolpyruvate\\n• Pyruvate is an α-keto acid that can be converted to glucose.\\n• Alanine is a glucogenic amino acid that is transaminated to form pyruvate by the enzyme alanine amino-transferase in the liver.\\n• Lactate\xa0obtained from anaerobic glycolysis is\xa0released from skeletal muscles and RBCs into the blood. This is taken up by the liver in the Cori cycle to be oxidised to pyruvate, to eventually synthesize glucose\\n• Glycerol from adipose tissue is converted to dihydroxyacetone phosphate in the liver, which itself is an intermediate of glycolysis and gluconeogenesis.\\n\\nThe steps and enzymes involved in gluconeogenesis are shown in blue in the flowchart below.","img":false,"explImg":true,"explBlocks":[{"t":"text"},{"t":"img","src":"/simg/1510ed7b2690962bd849.jpg"}]},{"n":27,"subject":"Biochemistry","text":"The echocardiography report of a 35-year-old woman hailing from China indicates dilated cardiomyopathy. Which of the following conditions can explain her diagnosis?","options":["Zinc toxicity","Selenium toxicity","Selenium deficiency","Zinc deficiency"],"correct":2,"explanation":"Correct answer: C. Selenium deficiency\\n\\nThe patient is probably suffering from Keshan\'s disease, which is due to selenium deficiency.\\n\\nKeshan disease is endemic cardiomyopathy found in children and young women residing in regions of China where dietary intake of selenium is low. Mainly dilated cardiomyopathy is characteristic, and may also result in cardiomegaly,\xa0congestive cardiac failure and fulminant cardiogenic shock.\\n\\nSelenium is an important trace element that is a part of the 21st amino acid selenocysteine. There are many selenium-containing enzymes such as:\\n\\n• Glutathione peroxidase\\n• De-iodinase\\n• Thioredoxin reductase\\n\\nSelenium is also an intracellular antioxidant that complements the role of vitamin E. Dietary sources of selenium include seafood, muscle meat, and\xa0some cereals.","img":false,"explImg":false},{"n":28,"subject":"Biochemistry","text":"Which of the following is true about lipogenesis stimulated by Insulin?","options":["Decreases acetyl CoA carboxylase activity","Decreases intracellular cAMP level","Decreases the transport of glucose into the cells","Inhibits PDH"],"correct":1,"explanation":"Correct answer: B. Decreases intracellular cAMP level\\n\\nInsulin\xa0decreases intracellular cAMP level to stimulate lipogenesis.\\n\\nInsulin stimulates lipogenesis by\\n\\n• Increasing acetyl-CoA carboxylase.\\n• Increases the transport of glucose into the cell.\\n• Activates pyruvate dehydrogenase\xa0in the adipose tissue (fatty acid synthesis and glycerol-3-phosphate for triacylglycerol synthesis via esterification of the newly formed fatty acids)\\n• Decreasing\xa0the level of intracellular cAMP\xa0(this inhibits lipolysis in adipose tissue, reducing the concentration of plasma-free fatty acids and, therefore, long-chain acyl-CoA, which are inhibitors of lipogenesis)","img":false,"explImg":false},{"n":29,"subject":"Biochemistry","text":"A baby was brought to the clinic with symptoms of diarrhea, failure to thrive, and hair loss. On examination, the following findings are noted. Identify the mineral deficiency leading to this condition.","options":["Niacin deficiency","Zinc deficiency","Riboflavin deficiency","Copper deficiency"],"correct":1,"explanation":"Correct answer: B. Zinc deficiency\\n\\nThe clinical features shown in the images are periorificial dermatitis and alopecia. These features\xa0in a young child are suggestive of acrodermatitis enteropathica seen with zinc deficiency.\\n\\nAcrodermatitis enteropathica\xa0is a condition\xa0that occurs due to zinc deficiency and presents after weaning.\xa0Clinical features include failure to thrive, anorexia, diarrhea, recurrent infections, alopecia, male hypogonadism, and neurological manifestations like tremor, nystagmus, photophobia, hypogeusia, irritability, etc.\\n\\nSkin manifestations such as vesiculobullous dermatitis on the extremities and periorificial(perianal, perigenital, perioral) regions are commonly seen as shown below. Affected infants require long-term supplementation with zinc.\\n\\nOption C- Riboflavin deficiency manifests as glossitis, cheilitis, magenta-colored tongue, angular stomatitis, and circumcorneal vascularization as shown below.\\n\\nOption D- Copper deficiency can present as anemia, cardiovascular defects including aneurysms, hypopigmentation, and neurological symptoms such as ataxia.","img":false,"explImg":true,"images":["/simg/69b9237b0a0b16dcd7a6.jpg"],"explBlocks":[{"t":"text"},{"t":"img","src":"/simg/94fc64d66c97c40d20cc.jpg"},{"t":"img","src":"/simg/df51754005cc83955700.jpg"},{"t":"img","src":"/simg/f0f55dcc46ebb37abf7b.jpg"}]},{"n":30,"subject":"Biochemistry","text":"Match the following mucopolysaccharidoses with the defective enzyme in each case:\\n\\nMucopolysaccharidosis\\nDefective enzyme\\n\\n1.\xa0Hunter syndrome\\nA. α-l-Iduronidase\\n\\n2. Morquio syndrome B\\nB. Iduronate sulfatase\\n\\n3.\xa0Hurler syndrome\\nC. Heparan sulfate-N-sulfatase\\n\\n4. Sanfilippo syndrome A\\nD. β-galactosidase","options":["1-B, 2-D, 3-A, 4-C","1-B, 2-A, 3-C, 4-D","1-A, 2-D, 3-C, 4-B","1-C, 2-B, 3-D, 4-A"],"correct":0,"explanation":"Correct answer: A. 1-B, 2-D, 3-A, 4-C\\n\\nThe option showing\xa01-B, 2-D 3-A, 4-C\xa0\xa0is correctly matched.\\n\\nMucopolysaccharidosis\\nDefective enzyme\\n\\n1. Hunter syndrome\\nB. Iduronate sulfatase\\n\\n2. Morquio syndrome B\\nD. β-galactosidase\\n\\n3. Hurler syndrome\\nA. α-l-Iduronidase\\n\\n4. Sanfilippo syndrome A\\nC. Heparan sulfate-N-sulfatase\\n\\nGlycosaminoglycans (GAGs) or mucopolysaccharides are complex carbohydrates made up of\xa0amino sugars and uronic acids. When they are attached to a protein they are referred to as proteoglycans. These proteoglycans make up a large part of the extracellular matrix and connective tissue.\\n\\nDefects in the enzymes that degrade GAGs result in the mucopolysaccharidoses mentioned above. These defects are commonly inherited\xa0in an autosomal recessive pattern. Accumulation of GAGs in the tissue can result in organomegaly, cartilage and bone abnormalities, facial abnormalities, problems with mental development, etc.","img":false,"explImg":false}]}')}}]);